\chapter*{How to use this book}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{How to use this book}
\markboth{How to use this book}{}
\setcounter{secnumdepth}{-1}

\section{What this book is}

This is a working guide to the family of regression models you reach for when your
outcome is \emph{not} a nice continuous measurement: yes/no outcomes, counts of
events, rates per unit of exposure, and any of those measured repeatedly on the
same people, animals, schools or clinics.

Everything is explained twice. Once in plain words, with a picture or an analogy.
Once in \R{} code that you can copy, run, and change. Every number and every plot
in this book was produced by actually running the code shown next to it, so what
you see on the page is what you will see in your console.

\begin{plainenglish}[The one-sentence summary of the whole book]
Ordinary linear regression assumes your outcome is a continuous number that can
wander anywhere and whose wobble is the same everywhere; when the outcome is a
yes/no or a count, both of those assumptions break, and this book is about the
family of models that fix them.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{Who it is for}

You should be comfortable with:

\begin{itemize}
  \item ordinary linear regression (\fn{lm}) --- what a slope means, what a
        $p$-value is, what a residual is;
  \item basic \R{} --- reading in data, \fn{data.frame}, \fn{\$}, \fn{summary}.
\end{itemize}

You do \emph{not} need calculus, matrix algebra, or any prior exposure to
generalised linear models. Where mathematics appears it is there to pin down an
idea that is already explained in words, and you can skip it on a first read
without losing the thread.

\section{How the chapters fit together}

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.06\textwidth}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.56\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Ch} & \textbf{Topic} & \textbf{What you get out of it} \\
\midrule
1  & The big picture           & A decision tree: which model for which outcome \\
2  & GLM anatomy               & The three moving parts every model here shares \\
3--4 & Logistic regression      & Yes/no outcomes, odds ratios, ROC, calibration \\
5--6 & Poisson regression       & Counts, rate ratios, offsets and exposure \\
7  & Overdispersion            & The single most common problem with count models \\
8  & Quasi-likelihood \& robust SEs & The quickest repair for overdispersion \\
9  & Negative binomial         & The proper likelihood-based repair \\
10 & Zero-inflation \& hurdles & When there are far too many zeros \\
11 & Other count models        & Truncation, underdispersion, ordinal, GAMs \\
12 & Model checking            & Residuals that actually work for counts \\
13 & Tests                     & LRT, Wald, score, AIC/BIC, Vuong \\
14--15 & Mixed models          & Repeated measures and clustering; GLMMs \\
16 & Marginal vs.\ conditional & Why GLMM and GEE give different numbers \\
17 & Convergence problems      & What the scary warnings mean and how to fix them \\
18 & A complete worked analysis & Everything applied end-to-end \\
19 & Cheat sheets              & Tables to keep beside you while you work \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

If you are in a hurry and you have count data, read Chapters 5, 7, 9 and 12. That
is the shortest path to a defensible analysis.

\section{Setting up \R}

Install the packages once:

<<install-pkgs, eval=FALSE>>=
install.packages(c("MASS", "lme4", "glmmTMB", "pscl", "AER",
                   "lmtest", "sandwich", "car", "emmeans", "ggplot2"))
@

and load the ones we use constantly:

<<load-pkgs, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
library(MASS)      # glm.nb(), and several teaching datasets
library(lme4)      # glmer(), lmer() for mixed models
library(glmmTMB)   # negative binomial / zero-inflated mixed models
library(pscl)      # zeroinfl(), hurdle()
library(AER)       # dispersiontest()
library(lmtest)    # coeftest(), lrtest()
library(sandwich)  # robust standard errors
@

\begin{plainenglish}[What each package is for, in one line each]
\pkg{MASS} adds the negative binomial. \pkg{lme4} adds random effects.
\pkg{glmmTMB} does both at once and adds zero-inflation. \pkg{pscl} does
zero-inflated and hurdle models without random effects. \pkg{AER} gives you a
formal overdispersion test. \pkg{lmtest} and \pkg{sandwich} give you likelihood-ratio
tests and robust standard errors.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{The datasets used in this book}

All of these ship with \R{} or with one of the packages above, so you can run
every example without downloading anything.

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.20\textwidth}p{0.11\textwidth}p{0.32\textwidth}p{0.29\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Package} & \textbf{Outcome variable} & \textbf{Used for} \\
\midrule
\fn{birthwt}          & MASS     & low birth weight (0/1)               & logistic regression \\
\fn{cbpp}             & lme4     & cases out of herd size               & binomial GLMM \\
\fn{quine}            & MASS     & days absent (count)                  & overdispersion, NB \\
\fn{warpbreaks}       & datasets & breaks (count)                       & Poisson basics \\
\fn{bioChemists}      & pscl     & articles published (count)           & zero-inflation, hurdles \\
\fn{RecreationDemand} & AER      & boating trips (count)                & extreme zero-inflation \\
\fn{epil}             & MASS     & seizures (count, repeated)           & count mixed models \\
\fn{grouseticks}      & lme4     & ticks on chicks (count, nested)      & Poisson GLMM + OLRE \\
\fn{sleepstudy}       & lme4     & reaction time (repeated)             & linear mixed models \\
\fn{InsectSprays}     & datasets & insects counted (count)              & simple Poisson \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{A note on reproducibility}

Anywhere this book simulates data, a seed is set first, so you will get exactly
the numbers printed here:

<<seed-demo>>=
set.seed(2024)
rpois(8, lambda = 3)
@

\section{Conventions}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Code appears in shaded boxes; output lines begin with \verb|#>|.
  \item Blue boxes translate an idea into plain English.
  \item Green boxes hold the key idea you should remember.
  \item Amber boxes hold rules of thumb --- useful, but not laws.
  \item Red boxes are mistakes people really make. Read those twice.
\end{itemize}

\begin{keyidea}[Try this]
Do not just read the code. Change a number in it, re-run it, and see what moves.
Almost everything in this book was learned by somebody breaking a model and then
working out why it broke.
\end{keyidea}

